1. Broken Access Control
Vulnerable Code
JavaScript
app.get('/profile/:userId', (req, res) => {
User.findById(req.params.userId, (err, user) => {
if (err) return res.status(500).send(err);
res.json(user);
});
});
Show more lines
What is wrong?

The program trusts the user ID in the URL. A person could change the ID and possibly see another person's profile.

OWASP specifically lists viewing or changing another person's account using its identifier as a Broken Access Control problem.

Secure Version
JavaScript
app.get('/profile/:userId', requireLogin, async (req, res) => {
if (req.user.id !== req.params.userId &&
req.user.role !== 'admin') {
return res.status(403).send('Access denied');
}
 
const user = await User.findById(req.params.userId);
res.json(user);
});
Show more lines
How I fixed it

I added a check that compares the logged-in user with the requested account. An admin is also allowed to access it.

If someone does not have permission, they get a 403 error.

2. Broken Access Control
Vulnerable Code
Python
@app.route('/account/<user_id>')
def get_account(user_id):
user = db.query(User).filter_by(id=user_id).first()
return jsonify(user.to_dict())
Show more lines
What is wrong?

This has almost the same problem as the first example. Someone can change the user_id and try to access somebody else's account.

Secure Version
Python
@app.route('/account/<user_id>')
@login_required
def get_account(user_id):
 
if current_user.id != user_id and current_user.role != "admin":
abort(403)
 
user = db.query(User).filter_by(id=user_id).first_or_404()
 
return jsonify(user.to_dict())
Show more lines
How I fixed it

I made the user log in first. Then the program checks if they own the account or if they are an admin.

This helps stop users from looking at information they should not have.

3. Cryptographic Failures
Vulnerable Code
Java
public String hashPassword(String password)
throws NoSuchAlgorithmException {
 
MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
md.update(password.getBytes());
 
byte[] digest = md.digest();
 
return DatatypeConverter.printHexBinary(digest);
}
Show more lines
What is wrong?

The program uses MD5 for password hashing.

OWASP includes broken or risky cryptographic algorithms under Cryptographic Failures.

Secure Version

A better approach is using a password-hashing library such as BCrypt.

Java
String hashedPassword = BCrypt.hashpw(
password,
BCrypt.gensalt()
);
Show more lines

To check the password:

Java
if (BCrypt.checkpw(inputPassword, hashedPassword)) {
System.out.println("Login successful");
}
Show more lines
How I fixed it

Instead of using MD5, I used a password hashing method made for passwords.

This gives the password better protection if someone gets access to the stored password hashes.

