I used a password-hashing function instead of directly using SHA-1.

The password can now be checked without storing the original password.

5. Injection
Vulnerable Code
Java
String username = request.getParameter("username");
 
String query =
"SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '" +
username + "'";
 
Statement stmt = connection.createStatement();
ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery(query);
Show more lines
What is wrong?

The username is being added directly into the SQL command.

This is dangerous because user input should not control how a database command works.

OWASP lists Injection as A03 in its 2021 Top 10.

Secure Version
Java
String username = request.getParameter("username");
 
String query =
"SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ?";
 
PreparedStatement stmt =
connection.prepareStatement(query);
 
stmt.setString(1, username);
 
ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery();
Show more lines
How I fixed it

I used a prepared statement.

The username is handled as data instead of being added directly into the SQL command. This helps protect the database from SQL injection.

6. Injection
Vulnerable Code
JavaScript
app.get('/user', (req, res) => {
 
db.collection('users').findOne(
{ username: req.query.username },
(err, user) => {
if (err) throw err;
res.json(user);
}
);
});
Show more lines
What is wrong?

The program trusts information coming from the user and sends it directly into the database query.

Secure Version
JavaScript
app.get('/user', async (req, res) => {
 
if (typeof req.query.username !== 'string') {
return res.status(400).send('Invalid username');
}
 
const username = req.query.username.trim();
 
const user = await db.collection('users')
.findOne({ username: username });
 
res.json(user);
});
Show more lines
How I fixed it

I checked that the username is actually a string before using it.

This limits the type of information that can be sent into the database query.

7. Insecure Design
Vulnerable Code
Python
@app.route('/reset-password', methods=['POST'])
def reset_password():
 
email = request.form['email']
new_password = request.form['new_password']
 
user = User.query.filter_by(email=email).first()
 
user.password = new_password
 
db.session.commit()
 
return 'Password reset'
Show more lines
What is wrong?

This program lets somebody reset a password just by providing an email address and a new password.

There is no proof that the person actually owns the account.

OWASP describes Insecure Design as problems involving missing or ineffective security controls in the application's design.

Secure Idea

I would change the design so the user first has to prove that they own the account.

A reset process could use a secure, temporary reset token before the password is changed.

The new password should also be hashed instead of being saved as plain text.

How I fixed it

The main fix is making password reset require verification before changing the password.

This adds an important security check that the original code was missing.

