The real hash would have to come from the trusted library being used. I would not make up a hash and use it in a real website.

9. Server-Side Request Forgery
Vulnerable Code
Python
url = input("Enter URL: ")
 
response = requests.get(url)
 
print(response.text)
Show more lines
What is wrong?

The program lets the user enter any URL and then makes the request for them.

This means the user controls where the server sends the request.

Secure Version
Python
allowed_urls = {
"weather": "https://trusted.example/weather"
}
 
choice = input("Enter service: ")
 
url = allowed_urls.get(choice)
 
if not url:
raise ValueError("URL is not allowed")
 
response = requests.get(url, timeout=5)
 
print(response.text)
Show more lines
How I fixed it

Instead of allowing any URL, I created a list of destinations the program is allowed to use.

The user chooses an approved service instead of giving the program any URL they want.

